  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Paul Coulson qualified as a chartered accountant after studying business at Trinity College Dublin. He established his own accounting practice, then created Yeoman International as an aircraft-leasing and structured-finance business. In 1998 Yeoman invested in the Irish Glass Bottle Company and Coulson became chairman. He used leveraged acquisitions to turn its single Dublin plant into Ardagh Group, a multinational producer of glass bottles and metal cans.\n\nArdagh expanded across Europe, the Americas and Africa, reaching annual revenue above $9 billion through more than twenty acquisitions. Coulson stepped down as chairman while retaining control and board roles, but heavy debt eventually forced a comprehensive 2025 recapitalisation. Bondholders converted debt into equity, ending his control and directorships in exchange for a substantial cash payment. Coulson's fortune reflects both the industrial scale he built and the financing strategy used to assemble it.",
    "html": "<p>Paul Coulson qualified as a chartered accountant after studying business at Trinity College Dublin. He established his own accounting practice, then created Yeoman International as an aircraft-leasing and structured-finance business. In 1998 Yeoman invested in the Irish Glass Bottle Company and Coulson became chairman. He used leveraged acquisitions to turn its single Dublin plant into Ardagh Group, a multinational producer of glass bottles and metal cans.</p>\r\n<p>Ardagh expanded across Europe, the Americas and Africa, reaching annual revenue above $9 billion through more than twenty acquisitions. Coulson stepped down as chairman while retaining control and board roles, but heavy debt eventually forced a comprehensive 2025 recapitalisation. Bondholders converted debt into equity, ending his control and directorships in exchange for a substantial cash payment. Coulson's fortune reflects both the industrial scale he built and the financing strategy used to assemble it.</p>\r\n",
